  [34mconst[39m DEFAULTS = {
    [36mrememberedUsername[39m: { [36mvalue[39m: [31m""[39m,      [36mvalid[39m: [33m([39mv) => [34mtypeof[39m v === [31m"string"[39m && v.length <= [32m256[39m },
    [36mtheme[39m:              { [36mvalue[39m: [31m"light"[39m, [36mvalid[39m: [33m([39mv) => v === [31m"light"[39m || v === [31m"dark"[39m },
  };

  Adding a preference later is one entry here and nothing else.

  On disk, one key holding one versioned blob:

  localStorage["webapp.prefs"] = '{"v":1,"rememberedUsername":"ada","theme":"dark"}'

  The factory takes its backend by injection:

  [34mfunction[39m [33mcreatePrefs[39m(storage) { ... }   [32m// defaults to globalThis.localStorage[39m

  returning four methods:

  - [38;5;153mget(key)[39m — the stored value if present and valid, else the default. Throws on an unknown key, because asking for a
    preference that doesn't exist is a typo, not a runtime condition to handle.
  - [38;5;153mset(key, value)[39m — validates against the registry, writes the merged blob. Throws on unknown key or invalid value.
  - [38;5;153mall()[39m — every preference, defaults filled in. Used for the one read at page load.
  - [38;5;153mclear()[39m — removes the key entirely. This is what "forget me" calls, and it's what keeps the tests isolated.

  Two deliberate choices worth flagging. [38;5;153mget[39m reads through to storage every call rather than caching, so a value written
  in another tab is picked up on the next read — that's the blob model's clobber weakness partly mitigated for free.
  And the version field [38;5;153mv:1[39m is written now but only [3mread[0m as a guard: unknown version means treat as absent and fall back
  to defaults. I'm not writing a migration framework for a schema with two fields; the field exists so a future
  migration has something to branch on.

  The one value that never enters this system is the password. [38;5;153mset("password", ...)[39m throws like any other unknown key —
  there's no registry entry to add it through without a deliberate code change.

  Does the data model look right?

